<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="chorismate biosynthesis from 3-dehydroquinate" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-6163" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Common Viridiplantae Pathways (CVP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most Viridiplantae species including algae. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Background&lt;/b&gt; In this pathway chorismate is synthesized from |FRAME: DEHYDROQUINATE|. The pathway is found in prokaryotes (both bacteria and archaea) and several eukaryotes, including ascomycete fungi, apicomplexans, plants and algae |CITS: [16963634]|. However, its starting point, |FRAME: DEHYDROQUINATE|, can be synthesized in different routes. In most microorganisms it is synthesized from |FRAME: ERYTHROSE-4P| (see |FRAME: PWY-6164|), but the methanogens of the |FRAME: TAX-28890| phylum utilize an alternative route |CITS: [15182204]| (see |FRAME: PWY-6160|). |FRAME: CHORISMATE &quot;Chorismate&quot;| is an intermediate in the synthesis of the three aromatic amino acids: |FRAME: PHE|, |FRAME: TYR| and |FRAME: TRP|. These amino acids serve as substrates in other pathways for secondary metabolites such as alkaloids, flavonoids, lignin, coumarin, indole derivatives and other phenolic compounds |CITS: [22296303]|. |FRAME: SHIKIMATE &quot;Shikimate&quot;| is an important intermediate in the chorismate biosynthetic pathway. It was first isolated from Japanese star anise. Shikimate is used for the production of some antiviral drugs used to halt the spread of the flu virus, but as a biosynthetic intermediate, it is usually not found in high concentrations in organisms. Shikimate can be easily harvested from the needles of several species of pine trees, and biosynthetic pathways in |FRAME:TAX-562| have been enhanced to obtain sufficient amounts of the metabolite for commercial use. &lt;b&gt;About this Pathway&lt;/b&gt; The first enzyme in this section of the pathway, |FRAME: EC-4.2.1.10|, dehydrates |FRAME: DEHYDROQUINATE| to |FRAME: 3-DEHYDRO-SHIKIMATE|, which is subsequently reduced to |FRAME: SHIKIMATE| by |FRAME: EC-1.1.1.25| (or in some organisms also by |FRAME: EC-1.1.1.282|). The next enzyme, |FRAME: EC-2.7.1.71|, was shown in |FRAME: TAX-1423| to form a complex with the bifunctional |FRAME: CPLX-1101| (|FRAME: G-279|). The complex may serve as a regulatory unit |CITS: [170268] [BSUB225]|. |FRAME: EC-2.5.1.19|, catalyzes the penultimate step in chorismate biosynthesis. The synthase is activated by ammonium ions and exhibits allosteric behavior. |FRAME: CPD0-1308 Glyphosate| acts as a competitive inhibitor with respect to |FRAME: PHOSPHO-ENOL-PYRUVATE| and as a partial uncompetitive inhibitor with respect to |FRAME: SHIKIMATE-5P| |CITS: [3111378] [7744055][7589547]|. The last enzyme of the pathway, |FRAME: EC-4.2.3.5|, catalyzes the conversion of |FRAME: 3-ENOLPYRUVYL-SHIKIMATE-5P| into |FRAME: CHORISMATE|, the key branch-point intermediate in aromatic biosynthesis.</Description>
